  "summary": "Today's health-tech landscape reflects several converging priorities. Data standardization and quality emerge as critical bottlenecks across multiple initiatives—from Spain's healthcare AI strategy to payers' ability to leverage AI for financial operations and the FDA's pending AI policy updates. Several stories underscore this reality: Dell and Microsoft partnerships, Sharecare's AWS integration, and recurring warnings that AI's effectiveness depends less on model sophistication than on foundational data infrastructure. On the clinical innovation front, AI is reshaping drug discovery approaches, at-home care delivery, and how hospitals deploy analytics, while the NIH's All of Us program is testing new data-sharing methods to accelerate research. Finally, regulatory and policy developments are shaping the industry's trajectory, including FDA leadership transitions, emerging questions around Trump administration drug pricing deals, and state-level actions on Medicaid and student athlete health requirements.",
